{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Settling the \"intermittent\" question: it is NOT intermittent. The 04:30 success was a paused no-op.\n\nAsked to check the reconciler's real query against what I measured, and to explain how a pass\ncould succeed at a working set of 1,388. Both are now answered, and the answer changes the\nincident's shape.\n\n### 1. The production JQL is exactly what I measured — no narrower filter\n\n`_build_snapshot` derives its project list from `projects_store.read_projects()`, falling back\nto `[query_project]` when the mapping is unseeded. The mapping is **empty** (`bridge projects\nlist` -> `{}`), and the workflow sets `JIRA_PROJECT: REB`, so the fan-out is the single project\nREB and the base queries are verbatim:\n\n```\njql_active(REB)      = project = REB AND statusCategory != \"Done\"       (cap None)\njql_done_recent(REB) = project = REB AND statusCategory = \"Done\" ORDER BY updated DESC  (cap 1000)\n```\n\nNo label, date or component filter anywhere. What I measured **is** the production query.\n\n### 2. The count is exact, not approximate\n\nRe-measured by **paging the REST API and counting rows**, not by `approximate-count`:\n\n| query | approximate-count | EXACT (paged) | delta |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `statusCategory != \"Done\"` | 1,388 | **1,388** (14 pages) | 0 |\n| `statusCategory = \"Done\"` | 2,992 | **2,992** (30 pages) | 0 |\n\nSo 1,388 is a true count.\n\n### 3. The 04:30 \"success\" never fetched anything — the bridge is PAUSED\n\nFrom run `33944760258`'s reconciler step:\n\n```\nBRIDGE_PAUSED: {\"paused\":true,\"reason\":\"repair:doctor:aca7242c-cd02-411b-931e-37b385f375a7\",\n                \"who\":\"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com\", ...}\nReconciling with bridge profile: live\nReconcile bridge is paused; skipping ticket commit and push.\n```\n\nIt ran for **one second** (04:34:46 -> 04:34:47) against ~21 minutes for a real pass (the 00:59\nsuccess ran 01:02:43 -> 01:23:51). It exited before the fetch. That is not a recovery; it is a\npause someone set, and it is why the schedule looks green again.\n\n**My own earlier hypothesis is disconfirmed, and I am recording that rather than quietly\ndropping it.** Before finding the pause I reasoned that a non-raising pass at 1,388 matched\nissues must mean the single `acli --paginate` call returned fewer than 1,200 rows — i.e. that\nthe \"successes\" were silently truncated reads. That would have been alarming and it is wrong.\nThe 04:30 run did not fetch at all.\n\n### 4. What actually happened: a single, permanent threshold crossing\n\nReconstructed the active count at each run by combining \"active now and created before T\" with\n\"Done now, created before T, moved to Done after T\". Both clauses were validated with controls\nfirst — and the first attempt was **wrong**, because JQL date literals resolve in the Jira\nuser's timezone (`America/Los_Angeles`), not UTC, so every timestamp I passed was in the future\nand every query silently returned the whole set. Redone with the offset applied:\n\n| run (UTC) | outcome | reconstructed active set |\n|---|---|---|\n| 00:59 | success | **1,193** — under the 1,200 ceiling |\n| 01:33 | FAILURE | 1,388 |\n| 02:34 | FAILURE | 1,388 |\n| 03:31 | FAILURE | 1,388 |\n| 04:30 | \"success\" | 1,388 — **but paused, no fetch** |\n\nAnd the cause of the crossing, measured directly:\n\n```\ncreated in REB between 17:59 and 18:33 local (= 00:59 -> 01:33 UTC):  200\n   of those, still active:                                            200\ncreated in REB since 18:33 local:                                       0\n```\n\n**Exactly 200 issues were created in that 34-minute gap**, taking the active set from 1,193 to\n1,388 — straight through the 1,200 ceiling — and **nothing has been created since**. The count\nhas been 1,388 for every run after the crossing.\n\nSo your loosely-held hypothesis was right about the mechanism (a burst of ticket creation\nduring that window) and it is worth saying it was a 200-issue burst rather than the ten you\nrecalled. But the conclusion drawn from the run history was the opposite of the truth: this is\na **permanent** crossing, not an intermittent one. Three real passes ran at 1,388 and all three\nfailed; the fourth did not run. Your original ticket framing — \"that headroom is now gone\" —\nwas correct, and the successes bracketing the failures are one genuine sub-ceiling pass before\nthe burst and one paused no-op after it.\n\nNothing here touches the fix direction, which you already said, and I agree: at 1,388 against a\n1,200 ceiling the coupling is exactly the defect, and the `created`-window partition removes it.\n\n### 5. A consequence worth flagging: the bridge is paused right now\n\n`repair:doctor:aca7242c-...` set by `joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com`. Until it is resumed, every\nhourly run is a one-second no-op and the bridge is reconciling nothing. I have **not** touched\nit — pausing/resuming is an operator action, and `bridge resume` is a mutating bridge op that I\nam not authorized to run. Flagging it because \"the schedule is green\" currently means \"the\nbridge is switched off\", which is worth knowing while judging this change.\n\n### 6. Proposed AC6 replacement (NOT edited — your call)\n\nAgreed that \"the Reconcile Bridge workflow returns to green on its hourly schedule\" is now\nunfalsifiable, and for a second reason beyond the one you gave: it is green *because the bridge\nis paused*. Proposed wording, for you to apply or reject:\n\n> - [ ] A reconciler pass completes without `SilentTruncationError` against an active working\n>       set **materially above** `_ACLI_CEILING` (>= 1,388 issues, the measured REB count at the\n>       time of the outage), and the resulting snapshot contains every matching issue. Proven\n>       by test at the fetch boundary; confirmed in production by the first unpaused live pass\n>       after this lands reporting a complete snapshot rather than by the workflow's exit code\n>       alone.\n\nThat cannot be satisfied by a pause, by a no-op, or by the set happening to dip back under the\nceiling.\n\n**I have not edited the description**, exactly because that would invalidate the plan-review\nattestation you just earned (`stale-material` against `currency_basis: file_impact`). Over to\nyou on whether to apply it and re-run the review knowingly.\n\nMy code changes stayed strictly inside the three paths already in `file_impact`, so the\nattestation is untouched by my work."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788583829148070625,"uuid":"6cafedb0-87ab-480d-98b2-df9f72515e14"}